<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bremen Roland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1404">1404</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> Roland is a stone statue of the paladin Roland, about 5.5 m tall, set up in 1404 in the market square of Bremen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, facing the cathedral. It symbolised the city's rights and freedoms against the archbishops of Bremen, and the inscription on its shield refers to the freedom granted to the city by the emperor. With the town hall it forms a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bremen-roland.jpg" alt="Bremen Roland" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jürgen Howaldt · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RolandBremen02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bremen Roland was erected in 1404 in the market square of Bremen, facing the cathedral. It was commissioned by the city fathers to replace a wooden statue burnt in 1366 by Prince-Archbishop Albert II. Statues of Roland, the paladin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Battle of Roncevaux Pass</a>, stand in numerous cities of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> as emblems of city liberties. The Bremen statue is the oldest surviving example, and from Bremen the symbol of civic freedom spread to other cities. It confronts the church as a representation of city rights opposed to the territorial claims of the prince-archbishop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Roland is shown as protector of the city, with his sword, known in chivalric legend as Durendal, unsheathed and a shield bearing the two-headed imperial eagle. The figure is 5.47 m tall and stands on a rostrum 60 cm high; a supporting column crowned by a baldachin brings the combined height to 10.21 m. It was carved in limestone from the Elm.</p><p>The inscription on the shield reads, in the original, vryheit do ik yu openbar, de karl und mennich vorst vorwar, desser stede ghegheven hat, des dankt gode is min radt. In English: Freedom I do manifest to you, which Karl and many noblemen indeed have given to this place; for this thank God, that is my advice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>According to legend, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> will remain free and independent as long as Roland keeps watch over the city. For this reason it is alleged that a second statue is kept hidden in the vaults of the town hall, ready to be installed should the original fall. The statue has been protected by the Monument Protection Act since 1973. In July 2004 it was added, together with the town hall, to the list of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, in recognition of its outstanding architecture and its symbolism of an important historical figure.</p><p>A smaller Roland, the Kleiner Roland, was erected in 1737 in Neustadt by the first Neustädter Bürgerkompanie, after the citizens of Neustadt had had very limited citizenship rights since 1642. It is based on the larger statue of 1404.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bremen Roland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
